{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>If a man's wife goes astray.</b> The juxtaposition of this pericope to the preceding verse Rashi explained: if you withhold the priest's gifts, your life will be such that you will need to come to him to bring him the sotah. According to Rashi's explanation, this is because the one who withholds the gifts will come to poverty, as Rashi explained above (Numbers 5:10), and our Sages of blessed memory said (Sotah 4a): everyone who lies with a forbidden woman will in the end seek a loaf of bread and not find it, as it says (Proverbs 6:26), 'כִּי בְעַד אִשָּׁה זוֹנָה עַד כִּכַּר לֶחֶם.' And when the wife sees that her husband has come to poverty, she suspects him — that he came to poverty because he was consorting with forbidden women, as it says (Proverbs 29:3), 'וְרֹעֶה זוֹנוֹת יְאַבֵּד הוֹן' — when she sees that suddenly his wealth is lost contrary to the way of the world; and for this reason she too will stray after him, so to speak: he is in his corner and she is in hers (Megillah 12b). Therefore it says 'and commit a trespass against him,' that if his wife goes astray and commits a trespass, then 'against him' — this trespass is dependent upon him, for he is the one who causes her to want to do as he does, from the side of suspicion. And all this he caused, by trespassing in the priest's holy things, by not giving the priest his due — for through this he comes to poverty, and she suspects him of consorting with forbidden women who destroy wealth; and the truth will be clarified through the examination of the waters, for our Sages of blessed memory said (Sotah 28a): 'וְנִקָּה הָאִישׁ מֵעָוֹן' — if he is clear of sin, the waters examine her, etc., which means: clear of this sin that resembles her sin, that if he is innocent of what his wife suspected him of, then it is just that the waters should examine her; but if he is not clear of this sin and her suspicion is true, it is not just that the waters should examine her, since he himself was consorting with forbidden women and she has some defense. And what it says, 'תִּשְׂטֶה' — the language of folly: even though every sinner is called a fool and a simpleton, nevertheless it specifies to say that she is especially steeped in naïveté, not knowing what caused him his poverty — for she should have understood that withholding the gifts caused him the poverty, as stated." "And according to the opinion of our Sages of blessed memory (Sotah 2a) that everyone who sees a sotah in her ruin should take a nazirite vow abstaining from wine, Scripture used the expression 'תִּשְׂטֶה' because wine confuses a person's intellect, as it is written (Hosea 4:11), 'זְנוּת וְיַיִן וְתִירוֹשׁ יִקַּח לֵב,' and from their juxtaposition to each other one hears from it that wine habituates to forbidden relations. Therefore it says in Joel (4:18), 'יִטְּפוּ הֶהָרִים עָסִיס' etc., and immediately after it, 'וּמַעְיָן יֵצֵא מִבֵּית ה׳ וְהִשְׁקָה אֶת נַחַל הַשִּׁטִּים' — because our Sages of blessed memory said (Numbers Rabbah 20:22) that the spring of Shittim produces adulterers, as it says (Numbers 25:1), 'וַיֵּשֶׁב יִשְׂרָאֵל בַּשִּׁטִּים וַיָּחֵל הָעָם לִזְנוֹת אֶל בְּנוֹת מוֹאָב.' Since it said 'יִטְּפוּ הֶהָרִים עָסִיס,' which means an abundance of wine, it was necessary to promise them that the abundance of wine would not harm them and would not bring them to habitual transgression — because 'a spring shall go forth from the house of YHWH and water the valley of Shittim,' for in every place where you find a barrier against forbidden relations, there you find holiness; therefore the spring would go forth from a holy place and heal the valley of Shittim so that it would not bring them to habitual transgression — it and its partner, namely the wine — for the valley of Shittim is the source of Samael, and he is a partner to the letters of עֲנָבִים in אַלְפָ״א בֵיתָ״א." "And were I not apprehensive to say something novel that the earlier authorities did not weigh, I would say that 'תִּשְׂטֶה' with a tet is like 'תִּשְׁתֶּה' with a tav, for tet interchanges with tav among the letters of the דַּטְלַנָ״ת group — and thus Rashi explained at the end of parashat Mikeitz (Genesis 44:16) on the verse 'וּמַה נִּצְטַדָּק,' that this tet is in place of a tav; and likewise the word 'תּוֹעֶה' throughout Scripture is with a tav, while in the language of our Sages of blessed memory, 'טוֹעֶה' with a tet. As if it said: when a man's wife drinks wine and strong drink that habituate to forbidden relations. And according to the one who says it is the language of folly — that is because wine takes hold of the heart until one does not know how to distinguish between holy and profane, as it is written (Leviticus 10:9–10), 'יַיִן וְשֵׁכָר אַל תֵּשְׁתְּ' etc. 'וּלְהַבְדִּיל' etc. And as it is written (Proverbs 31:5), 'פֶּן יִשְׁתֶּה וְיִשְׁכַּח מְחֻקָּק' — that is, the Torah; and it calls it by the name 'מְחֻקָּק' because it rises to the numerical value of חֶמֶר, and just as they said (Eiruvin 65a), 'wine enters, secret exits' — so too חֶמֶר enters, מְחֻקָּק exits: the calculation of this equals the calculation of that, and each is displaced by the other. And wine is called 'חֶמֶר' because through it a person becomes like a חֲמוֹר without understanding; therefore it says 'כִּי תִשְׂטֶה' — that she becomes a fool like a חֲמוֹר, to the point that even if a donkey demands her in the marketplace, she yields to it (Ketubot 65a)."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:40:05.482125141Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}